<!--METHOD
The data comes from the publicly published price lists of Slovak developers, which Residata reads daily, flat by flat. Supply consists of available and reserved flats in active projects; homes only — no parking, storage or commercial units. Prices and floor areas are means over the flats whose developer publishes a price, which is {natPricedShareEn}% of the offer. Price levels are computed as the mean of daily values over the last two weeks of the quarter, so that no single day on which a project added or withdrew part of its offer can move them. Months to clear is current supply divided by the monthly pace of sales in that quarter; it is shown only for towns with at least {natMinSales} sales, because below that it is one project's decision rather than a town's pace.
-->
{asOfEn} | QUARTERLY REVIEW OF THE SLOVAK NEW-BUILD MARKET

# Slovakia: one town clears its offer in {natFastestMoEn} months, another in {natSlowestMoEn}

At the close of the quarter the supply of new-build flats in Slovakia stood at {natStockEn} across {natTowns} towns. Average prices and the size of the offer differ between them, but the largest difference is not in price — it is in how fast that offer sells.

Bratislava accounts for {natBaShareEn}% of the whole offer and {natBaSoldShareEn}% of sales; the other {natRestTowns} towns share {natRestStockEn} flats between them. On price the range is a factor of {natPriceSpreadEn}: {natDearest} at {natDearestM2} €/m² incl. VAT against {natCheapest} at {natCheapestM2}.

Speed, however, does not follow price. At the current pace the offer in {natFastest} would clear in {natFastestMoEn} months and in {natSlowest} in {natSlowestMoEn} — a factor of {natSpreadEn}. {natFastest} averages {natFastestM2} €/m² incl. VAT and {natSlowest} {natSlowestM2}, which is lower. Across the {natWithClearing} towns where the pace can be computed, the relationship between price and clearing speed is effectively nil (a correlation of {natCorrEn}). What a town costs tells you nothing about how fast it sells.

The difference between towns is easier to see in floor area than in percentages. For the price of an average Bratislava flat — {natYardstickEn} € incl. VAT — you get {natYardstickSqm} m² in Bratislava. In {natBuy1} it is {natBuy1Sqm} m², in {natBuy2} {natBuy2Sqm} m² and in {natBuy3} {natBuy3Sqm} m².

